{"content":{"blocks":[{"content":"{\"additions\":9,\"author\":{\"id\":\"U_kgDOBsUTKg\",\"is_bot\":false,\"login\":\"chuoer47\",\"name\":\"辍二\"},\"body\":\"## Problem\\n\\nCloses #6.\\n\\nOn NVIDIA GPUs, Triton 3.1 uses TF32 by default for FP32 `tl.dot` operations, while the PyTorch reference path in this tutorial has TF32 disabled (`torch.backends.cuda.matmul.allow_tf32 == False`). The precision mismatch keeps the forward pass within tolerance, but the error accumulates and is amplified through the backward kernels, producing gradient errors up to ~0.72 and failing `assert_close(atol=5e-3)`.\\n\\n## Fix\\n\\nAdd `input_precision=\\\"ieee\\\"` to every fp32 `tl.dot` in both the forward and backward kernels (9 call sites) so the Triton matmuls match the PyTorch reference precision:\\n\\n- `_attn_fwd_inner`: `S = tl.dot(Q, K_T, ...)`, `O = tl.dot(P, V, acc=O, ...)`\\n- `_attn_backward_KV`: `S_T`, `dLdV`, `dLdP_T`, `dLdK`\\n- `_attn_backward_Q`: `S`, `dLdP`, `dLdQ`\\n\\nThis is an explicit, in-kernel fix (rather than relying on the `TRITON_F32_DEFAULT=ieee` env var), so the tests pass regardless of the user's default Triton FP32 precision.\\n\\n## Verification\\n\\nEnvironment: Triton 3.1.0, PyTorch 2.5.1+cu124, RTX 4090, `torch.backends.cuda.matmul.allow_tf32=False`, fresh Triton cache.\\n\\nAll 4 `test_flashattention_kernel` configurations pass forward + backward:\\n\\n| config (B, H, N, Dh) | fwd | bwd |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| (1, 1, 128, 32)   | passed | passed |\\n| (1, 1, 128, 64)   | passed | passed |\\n| (1, 1, 128, 128)  | passed | passed |\\n| (32, 8, 69, 128)  | passed | passed |\\n\\nMax gradient diffs (config 1, 1, 128, 32), down from ~0.72 before the fix:\\n\\n```\\nfwd max diff: 1.37e-06\\ndQ max diff: 3.43e-07\\ndK max diff: 4.62e-07\\ndV max diff: 1.79e-07\\n```\\n\\nAll diffs are now below 1e-6, consistent with the IEEE recompilation results reported in the issue.\\n\",\"deletions\":9,\"files\":[{\"path\":\"09_flash_attention/flash_attention.py\",\"additions\":9,\"deletions\":9,\"changeType\":\"MODIFIED\"}],\"state\":\"OPEN\",\"title\":\"fix(flash-attn): force IEEE precision on tl.dot for FP32 correctness\"}","is_error":false,"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EU4HRenkUe26cFZf9irfSa","type":"tool_result"}],"role":"user"},"context":{"branch":"main","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/deprecated/triton_docs_tutorials","dirty_fingerprint":"126c4977a00f41293cb34f75913ba953e022e70c8c3a3d7a5586ae0036745547","head":"1d79d23423ffb9f2853dddbeb22aabb82dca8af8","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/triton_docs_tutorials.git"},"id":"ev2-1bc9c5608d1e3927525ea388f351c397b9060791d27685b8c3f1b4523c796b65","kind":"conversation_turn","meta":{"actor":{"display":"Evin Tunador","id":"evintunador@gmail.com","type":"human"},"session_id":"c8f120f6-7e7f-406a-837c-50af362cae2c","source":"claude-code","source_version":"2.1.263"},"occurred_at":"2026-09-08T14:52:49.707Z","producer":{"tool":"cledger","version":"0.26.0"},"raw":{"data":{"cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/deprecated/triton_docs_tutorials","entrypoint":"cli","gitBranch":"main","isSidechain":false,"message":{"content":[{"content":"{\"additions\":9,\"author\":{\"id\":\"U_kgDOBsUTKg\",\"is_bot\":false,\"login\":\"chuoer47\",\"name\":\"辍二\"},\"body\":\"## Problem\\n\\nCloses #6.\\n\\nOn NVIDIA GPUs, Triton 3.1 uses TF32 by default for FP32 `tl.dot` operations, while the PyTorch reference path in this tutorial has TF32 disabled (`torch.backends.cuda.matmul.allow_tf32 == False`). The precision mismatch keeps the forward pass within tolerance, but the error accumulates and is amplified through the backward kernels, producing gradient errors up to ~0.72 and failing `assert_close(atol=5e-3)`.\\n\\n## Fix\\n\\nAdd `input_precision=\\\"ieee\\\"` to every fp32 `tl.dot` in both the forward and backward kernels (9 call sites) so the Triton matmuls match the PyTorch reference precision:\\n\\n- `_attn_fwd_inner`: `S = tl.dot(Q, K_T, ...)`, `O = tl.dot(P, V, acc=O, ...)`\\n- `_attn_backward_KV`: `