{"content":{"blocks":[{"text":"**2080 Ti result — and it's a bigger finding than expected.** My prediction was wrong in an informative way: I expected `main` to simply pass on Turing (falling back to FMA). Instead:\n\n| variant | 2080 Ti (Turing sm_75), torch 2.6.0 / triton 3.2.0 |\n|---|---|\n| `main` (default precision) | **compiler crash — all 4 configs** |\n| `main` + `TRITON_F32_DEFAULT=ieee` | **PASS**, max diffs ~1e-7 |\n| `pr7` | **PASS**, max diffs ~1e-7 |\n\n`main` doesn't produce wrong numbers on Turing — it fails to compile at all:\n\n```\nIndexError: map::at\n  triton/backends/nvidia/compiler.py:286 in make_llir → pm.run(mod)\n```\n\nTuring has no TF32 tensor cores, so when Triton 3.2.0 tries to lower a TF32 `tl.dot` for sm_75 it dies in the LLVM-IR lowering pass. So **on the 2080 Ti this PR isn't merely \"no regression\" — it converts a hard crash into a passing test.** The tutorial is currently unrunnable on Turing.\n\nTwo things worth flagging from this:\n\n1. **The PR's 9 sites are complete.** `pr7` produces numerically *identical* results to setting `TRITON_F32_DEFAULT=ieee` globally (byte-for-byte the same diffs across all 4 configs). That means the author didn't miss any `tl.dot` — a good independent check on the patch's thoroughness.\n2. **This is a plausible explanation for your README's \"older GPUs give incorrect values\" note** — though not fully, since the 3090 is Ampere and *does* have TF32. The 3090 result will settle that.\n\n3090 and 4090 are still running. I'll report when they land.","type":"text"}],"role":"assistant"},"context":{"branch":"main","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/deprecated/triton_docs_tutorials","dirty_fingerprint":"126c4977a00f41293cb34f75913ba953e022e70c8c3a3d7a5586ae0036745547","head":"1d79d23423ffb9f2853dddbeb22aabb82dca8af8","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/triton_docs_tutorials.git"},"id":"ev2-154046b54c8b85c593c74e64dd2aaf3f85326807fb558177f06d4c0bf51cee18","kind":"conversation_turn","meta":{"actor"