{"content":{"blocks":[{"text":"**4090 (Ada sm_89), torch 2.6.0 / triton 3.2.0 — your pinned stack:**\n\n| variant | fwd max diff | dQ / dK / dV max diff | verdict |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `main` (default = TF32) | 1.9e-3 … **3.6e-3** | 3.3e-4 … 6.9e-4 | **PASS** (all 4 configs) |\n| `main` + `TRITON_F32_DEFAULT=ieee` | 8.3e-7 … 3.8e-6 | 1.3e-7 … 7.5e-7 | PASS |\n| `pr7` | 8.3e-7 … 3.8e-6 | 1.3e-7 … 7.5e-7 | PASS |\n\nThree conclusions:\n\n**1. The PR's mechanism is real.** Triton does default fp32 `tl.dot` to TF32 — forcing IEEE drops forward error from 2.3e-3 to 8.3e-7, a ~2800× improvement. That half of the diagnosis is correct and now directly measured.\n\n**2. The PR's headline claim does not reproduce.** The reporter cited gradient errors up to ~0.72 causing test failure. On a real 4090 with your pinned stack, `main` gradient errors are **3.3e-4 to 6.9e-4** — roughly **1000× smaller** than reported, and all four configs pass. This matches the arithmetic I did earlier: TF32's 10-bit mantissa can only produce ~1e-3-scale error here, so 0.72 was never explicable by TF32. Their number has some other cause (their 4×4090 setup, a modified test, or the older Triton — testing that now).\n\n**3. There's still a genuine argument for merging, just not the stated one.** On config `(32,8,69,128)` the forward error is **3.6e-3 against an atol of 5e-3** — consuming 73% of the tolerance budget. The repo's own test seeds nothing (`torch.randn` with no `manual_seed`), so the margin is redrawn every run. That's a flaky test waiting to happen, and the PR removes the flakiness entirely by taking the error to ~1e-6.\n\nCombined with the Turing crash fix, the PR looks worth taking — but the PR description as written is misleading and shouldn't be merged with that justification unrebutted.\n\nStill pending: the 3090, and the Triton 3.1.0 rebuild on the 4090 that should explain the reporter's numbers.","type":"text"}],"role":"assistant"},"context":{"branch":"main","cwd":"/Users/evintuna