{"content":{"blocks":[{"text":"**The reporter's failure reproduces — but only on Triton 3.1.0.** Same 4090, same seed, same code, only the Triton version changed:\n\n| config | fwd max diff | dQ | dK | dV | |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| (1,1,128,32) | 2.321e-3 | 0.136 | 0.163 | 0.280 | FAIL |\n| (1,1,128,64) | 2.323e-3 | 0.237 | 0.166 | 0.357 | FAIL |\n| (1,1,128,128) | 1.941e-3 | 0.348 | 0.328 | 0.603 | FAIL |\n| (32,8,69,128) | 3.638e-3 | **3.34** | **5.91** | **3.89** | FAIL |\n\nCompare to Triton 3.2.0 on the *same GPU*: gradients 3.3e-4 to 6.9e-4, all passing.\n\nThe critical detail is the forward column. **Forward errors are bit-identical across Triton 3.1.0 and 3.2.0** — 2.321e-3, 2.323e-3, 1.941e-3, 3.638e-3 in both. So TF32 is being used identically in both versions and the forward path behaves identically. Yet the backward gradients differ by a factor of ~1000.\n\nThat rules out the PR's stated mechanism. If this were \"TF32 error accumulating and being amplified through the backward kernels,\" Triton 3.2.0 would show it too — same TF32 inputs, same math, same forward error. It doesn't. And a **5.91 absolute error on dK**, where the true values are O(0.1), is 60× the magnitude of the quantity itself — that is not rounding, that's a wrong answer. Errors also explode with problem size (0.28 → 0.60 → 3.89) rather than growing gently, which is the signature of a miscompilation, not accumulated precision loss.\n\n**Actual conclusion: Triton 3.1.0 miscompiles the TF32 backward kernels. That was fixed in Triton 3.2.0 — the version your repo already pins.** `input_precision=\"ieee\"` \"fixes\" it only because it steers codegen away from the buggy TF32 path.\n\nSo, three independent findings, all reproduced on real hardware:\n\n| environment | `main` | `pr7` |\n|---|---|---|\n| 4090 + triton 3.2.0 (**your pin**) | PASS (margin 73% consumed) | PASS, ~1000× headroom |\n| 4090 + triton 3.1.0 (reporter's) | **FAIL**, grads to 5.91 | PASS |\n| 2080 Ti + triton 3.2.0 | **compile crash** | PASS |\n\n**The PR is worth merging, but not for the reason it claims.** It's a robustness fix — it makes the tutorial correct on older Triton and compilable on pre-Ampere hardware — not a bug fix for your pinned stack, where the tests already pass.\n\n3090 pending on a replacement host (the first one wedged pulling Docker layers). Spend so far ≈ $0.35.","type":"text"}],"role":"assistant"},"context":{"branch":"main","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/deprecated/triton_docs_tutorials","dirty_fingerprint":"126c4977a00f41293cb34f75913ba953e022e70c8c3a3d7a5586ae0036745547","head":"1d79d23423ffb9f2853dddbeb22aabb82dca8af8","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/triton_docs_tutorials.git"},"id":"ev2-7791be1b7d85a5b7b393e379068882d41e3d36429ff8593b9c9cc27b83902ef5","kind":"conversation_turn","meta":{"actor":{"id":"claude-opus-5","type":"agent"},"model":"claude-opus-5","session_id":"c8f120f6-7e7f-406a-837c-50af362cae2c","source":"claude-code","source_version":"2.1.263"},"occurred_at":"2026-09-08T15:55:54.173Z","producer":{"tool":"cledger","version":"0.26.0"},"raw":{"data":{"apiBlockIndex":0,"cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/deprecated/triton_docs_tutorials","effort":"high","entrypoint":"cli","gitBranch":"main","isSidechain":false,"message":{"content":[{"text":"**The reporter's failure reproduces — but only on Triton 3.1.0.** Same 4090, same seed, same code, only the Triton version changed:\n\n| config | fwd max diff | dQ | dK | dV | |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| (1,1,128,32) | 2.321e-3 | 0.136 | 0.163 | 0.280 | FAIL |\n| (1,1,128,64) | 2.323e-3 | 0.237 | 0.166 | 0.357 | FAIL |\n| (1,1,128,128) | 1.941e-3 | 0.348 | 0.328 | 0.603 | FAIL |\n| (32,8,69,128) | 3.638e-3 | **3.34** | **5.91** | **3.89** | FAIL |\n\nCompare to Triton 3.2.0 on the *same GPU*: gradients 3.3e-4 to 6.9e-4, all passing.\n\nThe critical detail is the forward column. **Forward errors are bit-identical across Triton 3.1.0 and 3.2.0** — 2.321e-3, 2.323e-3, 1.941e-3, 3.6